{"connection_id": "BC-f83eedfee1991ac08cec", "ref": "Augustine, Confessions 10.29.40", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Obedience depends on grace", "note": "While reflecting on chastity, Augustine asks God to grant what God commands; this makes the commentary's pairing of worship and help a claim about moral action itself needing divine support."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d7ffc61ffe26b82abee7", "ref": "Augustine, Confessions 10.29.40", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "ask God to give what God commands", "note": "Augustine asks God to give what God commands before commanding what God wills; it is a classic Christian interpretation of obedience as requiring the very aid the worshiper petitions for."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c373b54b6d30b6748816", "ref": "Augustine, Quaestiones Evangeliorum 2.19", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "The Samaritan as Christ", "note": "Augustine reads the wounded traveler as fallen humanity and the Samaritan as Christ; this Christian interpretation shifts Luke's practical aid scene toward divine rescue, a useful contrast with the commentary's human shoulders and support."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b28fab847650a9de78af", "ref": "Didache 1.1", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the Way of Life and the Way of Death", "note": "The Didache opens with the Way of Life and the Way of Death; it supplies an early Christian ethical development of the path metaphor, though its two-way scheme goes beyond this section's single petition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f2c71fc9fe210be737e4", "ref": "Didache 1:1", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Two ways", "note": "The Didache opens by naming a way of life and a way of death; it gives the commentary's requested way a concrete ethical alternative in early Christian teaching."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f81ffc32145dec89693d", "ref": "Didache 1:5", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Aid for whoever asks", "note": "The Didache instructs its hearer to give to everyone who asks; within its teaching on the way of life, it makes the commentary's image of receiving support into a concrete obligation to support others."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2dcf4b7203dc6a787f1c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.12.22", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weaker members of the body are indispensable", "note": "The body's apparently weaker members are indispensable; it deepens the commentary's body-and-support image by making weakness a condition for mutual care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ac6d2f26a840a4ee6c0", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.5.10", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "restore, strengthen, establish, and found", "note": "God restores, strengthens, establishes, and founds believers after suffering; the cluster gives the source's wall, pillar, and uprightness images a Christian form."}
{"connection_id": "BC-73173e2793af6c05dc51", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.14", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Support the weak", "note": "Paul tells the community to help the weak and be patient with everyone; this makes the commentary's two-sided human support an ethical duty within a worshipping community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-23cd774b8d577527c86d", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.14", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "help the weak; be patient", "note": "The community is told to help the weak and be patient with all; it pairs the source's assistance with the unhurried disposition of good walking."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d104362d852fbb1f335b", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.3.15", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pillar and foundation of truth", "note": "The church is called the pillar and foundation of truth; it gives the commentary's qiyam-as-pillar image an ecclesial structural use."}
{"connection_id": "BC-174e7e813d00cefd40b9", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.12.9", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "power perfected in weakness", "note": "Christ's power is made perfect in weakness; it supplies a theological reading in which the source's weakness remains the place where enabling aid is displayed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-67ea58f70f893f36ba6b", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.4.17", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the Lord stood by and strengthened me", "note": "The Lord stands by Paul and strengthens him in abandonment; it gives the commentary's needed support a personal divine companion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fb8f9065d987e8600a3e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.14.10", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "stand upright and walk", "note": "Paul commands a man disabled in his feet to stand upright, and he leaps and walks; its wording explicitly joins uprightness to restored motion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3479933a7defc08148aa", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.16.9", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "come over and help us", "note": "A Macedonian man asks Paul to cross over and help them; the request makes aid both communal and tied to a journey across a boundary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-11f86bf3e457268a0e9b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.31", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "An appointed day of judgment", "note": "Paul says God fixed a day to judge the world in righteousness through an appointed man; this directly elaborates the judgment-day claim in the selected ayah."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bb33613a515e8790a9e0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.20.35", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "help the weak", "note": "The elders are reminded that one must help the weak; this concise imperative provides a communal form of the source's avn, help."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1da17c7012a6af36859c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.27.17", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "supports used to undergird a ship", "note": "In a storm the sailors use supports to undergird their ship; this gives the commentary's avn as what one relies on a literal structural and travel-related enactment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0274cad7bbdf307c37a7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.3.7", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A hand raises the lame man", "note": "Peter takes the lame man by the right hand and raises him; his feet and ankles become strong and he walks, making the commentary's transition from aid to upright walking an enacted healing scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-793a1eb62a4d5105e640", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.3.7", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "right hand raises; feet strengthened", "note": "Peter takes the lame man by the right hand and raises him, and his feet and ankles become strong; it gives the source's hand-held help a concrete upright result."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8b9c1d5332b28b14dfc4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.3.8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "leaps up, stands, and walks", "note": "The healed man leaps up, stands, and walks; this provides the full bodily sequence that the commentary reads into aid followed by upright movement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f89130ccd935b7011ceb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.8.31", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A reader asks for someone to guide him", "note": "The Ethiopian official says he cannot understand Isaiah unless someone guides him; this shifts the commentary's dependent traveler's need for guidance to interpretation, showing why a guide may be needed even by a capable traveler."}
{"connection_id": "BC-03c4275bc0589eb49f34", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.8.31", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "How can I unless someone guides me?", "note": "The Ethiopian traveler asks how he can understand unless someone guides him and invites Philip into the chariot; it turns guidance into companioned instruction on a journey."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f9f6759645ef1c215466", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.1.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "strength for endurance and patience", "note": "Believers are strengthened with power for endurance and patience; it gives the source's aided, unhurried walking an inward ethical correlate."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8e00d43cfb5424c856ed", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.2.7", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rooted, built up, established", "note": "Believers are rooted, built up, and established in faith; it brings together the commentary's tree-on-roots and stable-wall images."}
{"connection_id": "BC-46da394c453a346abe53", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.4.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "body held together by supporting joints", "note": "The whole body is joined and held together through every supporting joint; it translates the commentary's body-support image into reciprocal communal cohesion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8de207ef677bdd0bf0bc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.2", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Bearing one another's burdens", "note": "Paul commands believers to carry one another's burdens; this gives the commentary's image of leaning on another person an ethical, shared form."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b677f4d604f2f3adb50d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.2", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bear one another's burdens", "note": "Bearing one another's burdens makes bodily support an ethic of communal walking rather than an isolated rescue."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d18deb2fe6c3cbc7978f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.21", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Jacob worships leaning on his staff", "note": "Hebrews recounts Jacob blessing Joseph's sons and worshiping while leaning on his staff, following the Greek reading of Genesis 47:31; it makes worship and physical support occur together, exactly the pairing developed in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a5a2a4c1e3aadd7b1aa7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.21", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "worship while leaning on a staff", "note": "Jacob worships while leaning on the top of his staff; it brings worship, bodily support, and the vulnerable traveler's staff into a single Christian-scriptural image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-beef31ad4d616b487e4b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.12", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Drooping hands and weak knees", "note": "The author urges readers to lift drooping hands and strengthen weakened knees; this directly names the bodily frailty behind the commentary's request for help."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5c0f8c4a030ca10e7814", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.12", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "drooping hands; weak knees", "note": "The exhortation to lift drooping hands and strengthen weak knees takes up the same weakened limbs that the commentary imagines before aid."}
{"connection_id": "BC-be9810d79e847428f515", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Straight paths for weak feet", "note": "The author tells the community to make straight paths for their feet so the lame may be healed; it closely joins the commentary's weak walker, straight way, and restored gait."}
{"connection_id": "BC-203d2c1cf91f780cefcd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "straight paths for feet; lame healed", "note": "Straight paths are to be made for the feet so that the lame may be healed rather than dislocated; it is one of the closest Christian fusions of straight path, gait, and restoration."}
{"connection_id": "BC-351ffa10e50f71e1e12a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.13.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The Lord is the helper", "note": "The author quotes the confession that the Lord is one's helper and therefore one need not fear; it gives a direct first-person expression of reliance, though it does not develop the travel image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c4ee9c83683b0d1b17fb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.2.18", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "able to help those being tested", "note": "Because Jesus suffered when tested, he is able to help those tested; it offers a Christological account of help arising from shared vulnerability."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ed8b8ac120303b52c2d2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.4.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Help at the throne", "note": "Readers are invited to approach the throne of grace for mercy and timely help; the passage gives a prayerful counterpart to worship joined with asking for aid, and recasts approach to divine authority as confidence in receiving support."}
