{"connection_id": "BC-a32fce77fc0d90d298d1", "ref": "Sirach 37:15", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pray that the Most High direct your way", "note": "The sage tells the reader to pray that the Most High direct the way in truth; it is a close Jewish wisdom analogue to asking for the straight path."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6aa9a6179df2aa84ec18", "ref": "Sirach 4:11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wisdom helps those who seek her", "note": "Wisdom instructs her children and gives help to those who seek her; it explicitly joins sought assistance to instruction and guidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dda8fb50d1517a4a9f70", "ref": "Sotah 14a", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "walking after God means acts of care", "note": "The talmudic reading of walking after God turns the command into clothing, visiting, comforting, and burying the vulnerable; it makes walking with God inseparable from supporting the weak."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c50c3144d06c5dea8b01", "ref": "Tobit 5:7", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "traveler seeks someone who knows the road", "note": "Raphael says he knows the roads to Media and has traveled them often; Tobias's journey turns the request for guidance into finding a competent companion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5ce918a1bd7d8df2219b", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.19.7", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A journey too great", "note": "An angel returns, touches Elijah, and tells him to rise and eat because the journey is too much for him; this verse recognizes dependence before onward travel, matching the commentary's need for aid."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fbc3eb6ce23ebcac7449", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.19.7", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "q-w-m B002; journey too much", "note": "The angel tells exhausted Elijah to arise and eat because the journey is too much for him; Hebrew qum gives the source's q-w-m standing image a traveler-specific setting of divine provision."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8dde47ab913d312a09d7", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.19.8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Strength to continue", "note": "After eating, Elijah rises and travels forty days in the strength of that food; this gives a concrete result for the aid sought by the commentary's weakened traveler: renewed ability to walk onward."}
{"connection_id": "BC-09ffe1623b443a36852c", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.19.8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "strength to continue walking", "note": "Strengthened by the food, Elijah travels forty days to Horeb; this realizes the commentary's sequence in which help restores a body for the road."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3e75959a7eab5a67f645", "ref": "WLC:2Chr.14.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "help the weak against the mighty", "note": "Asa asks God to help the powerless against the mighty because none is like God in helping; this makes the source's request for aid an explicit prayer from weakness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8d14acc70a0d85c4a8e1", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.22.34", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "feet made like a deer's; secure heights", "note": "God gives the speaker agile feet and sets him securely on heights; it reframes restored walking as divinely given bodily surety."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a1db01108cda04e94024", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.22.36", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "right hand supports the speaker", "note": "God's right hand supports the speaker; it is a close biblical counterpart to the commentary's claim that aid is a support under a vulnerable person."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d091f8d5ccade320a91b", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.22.37", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "broad footing; ankles do not slip", "note": "God makes room for the speaker's steps so the ankles do not give way; it specifies secure footing as the result of help."}
{"connection_id": "BC-607f3a01b9c7c2b4955a", "ref": "WLC:Dan.10.18", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "touch that strengthens a weakened body", "note": "A humanlike figure touches the overwhelmed Daniel and strengthens him; this supplies a bodily counterpart to the source's aid turning weakness into steadiness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d409d8569ed89d92c56c", "ref": "WLC:Dan.10.19", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "peace and strength received after speech", "note": "The messenger says peace and be strong, and Daniel reports being strengthened; it adds spoken reassurance to the commentary's supported, non-panicked recovery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f9c90a05919f199008a7", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.10", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The heavenly court sits", "note": "The Ancient of Days takes his seat, the court sits, and books are opened; this is an apocalyptic judgment scene to place beside the selected ayah's day of reckoning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-15b15aadedff7b5d3a02", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.14", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Everlasting dominion after judgment", "note": "After the court scene, one like a son of man receives everlasting dominion and all peoples serve him; this places universal service beside the commentary's sovereign and worship, while Daniel distinguishes the enthroned judge from the figure given dominion."}

