{"connection_id": "BC-ac3c5fb1928c32ab0d6b", "ref": "WLC:Deut.1.31", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Carried on the journey", "note": "Moses recalls that YHWH carried Israel through the wilderness as a man carries his son; the traveling people depend on support through the whole route, extending the commentary's individual image to a community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aa96410123e4f950ed9f", "ref": "WLC:Deut.10.12", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "'ayn-b-d B007/B011; walk all God's ways", "note": "Deuteronomy couples walking in all God's ways with serving God from the whole heart; Hebrew avad is cognate with the source label for na'budu and preserves its worship-and-way sequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-111093b63b0022b080b7", "ref": "WLC:Deut.22.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fallen donkey on the road; lift it up", "note": "The law forbids ignoring a fellow Israelite's fallen donkey on the road and requires raising it with the owner; it directly answers the source's stopped mount with shared assistance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d46a87a347ca965795c", "ref": "WLC:Deut.27.18", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "curse on making a blind person stray", "note": "The curse falls on one who makes a blind person wander off the road; it casts the source's needed guidance as a communal obligation not to misdirect the vulnerable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3328d60b2049f73ffe23", "ref": "WLC:Deut.31.8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The guide goes before and does not abandon", "note": "Moses tells Joshua that YHWH goes before him and will not fail or forsake him; it makes the sought guidance personal and reassuring for someone unable to proceed alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-85c76df346f277157eea", "ref": "WLC:Deut.33.27", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "everlasting arms underneath", "note": "God's everlasting arms are beneath Israel; this gives the source's image of being borne from the sides a protective, sustaining underside."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ae45d000f277194d8e3a", "ref": "WLC:Deut.8.15", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A perilous wilderness journey", "note": "Moses recalls YHWH leading Israel through a great, dangerous wilderness and bringing water from flint; the community's physical dependence on guidance and sustenance closely expands the commentary's tired traveler."}
{"connection_id": "BC-54db8d611620a1865c8c", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.4.10", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A companion lifts the fallen", "note": "The verse says that if one falls, the other can raise his companion; it makes explicit the human help implicit in the commentary's walker leaning on two people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d22a31dfd68a7e75a892", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.4.10", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one falls; companion lifts", "note": "When one person falls, the other lifts the companion; it supplies the social logic behind the source's weak traveler being held rather than left alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6c2e86e205929912b74c", "ref": "WLC:Exod.13.21", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pillar leading the way", "note": "The LORD goes before Israel in a pillar to lead the way; it brings together the commentary's guide, road, and qiyam-as-pillar imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d45ded9dfc22f24245de", "ref": "WLC:Exod.15.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Guiding the redeemed by strength", "note": "In the Song at the Sea, YHWH leads the people he redeemed and guides them by his strength to his holy dwelling; it joins rescue, help, and a destination on the journey the commentary asks God to guide."}
{"connection_id": "BC-45b7c727bf7166165b59", "ref": "WLC:Exod.17.12", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Two-sided support", "note": "When Moses' hands grow heavy, Aaron and Hur seat him and hold them up, one on each side, until sunset; the commentary's exhausted walker is likewise sustained between companions, making dependence a shared act."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3a370329927f5a7b8b14", "ref": "WLC:Exod.17.12", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "heavy hands; two supporters", "note": "Moses' hands become heavy, and Aaron and Hur hold them up from opposite sides; this is the commentary's concrete image of a failing body steadied by people on both sides."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7fc63fc4adaa3aea356e", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.25", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "'ayn-b-d B007/B011; service and bodily healing", "note": "Israel is to serve God, who blesses bread and water and removes sickness; the Hebrew avad cognate places service beside the bodily strengthening the commentary associates with aid."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5a93b8f69823bcfd98b9", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.5", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey collapsed under a burden", "note": "The law requires helping even an enemy's donkey when it lies under its load; it gives the commentary's exhausted mount an ethical command not to leave it stranded."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1f5bea7dff269df3ef93", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.12", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ע־ב־ד, service to God", "note": "The source commentary labels Arabic ع ب د for نَعْبُدُ; Hebrew תַּעַבְדוּן (ta'avdun, root עבד) is its cognate in God's promise that liberated Israel will serve him at the mountain, adding the setting of worship as service after deliverance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9885579ce90575c4914a", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.2.2", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "spirit sets the hearer on his feet", "note": "The spirit enters Ezekiel and sets him on his feet while God speaks; it is a direct scene in which enabling presence produces upright posture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c3e40e3178f6e4479fea", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.34.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bind injured; strengthen weak", "note": "The shepherd seeks the lost, binds the injured, and strengthens the weak; it converts the source's individual supporting act into pastoral responsibility."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e3260ada31c4b48e2c30", "ref": "WLC:Ezra.8.21", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "we seek from God a straight way", "note": "Ezra proclaims a fast so the traveling community may seek from God a straight way for themselves, their children, and their goods; it closely joins communal petition, dependence, and the requested road."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f7a5f79523906e190aa0", "ref": "WLC:Ezra.8.22", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's hand helps travelers on the road", "note": "Ezra contrasts royal military help with God's favorable hand on those who seek him while facing enemies on the way; it gives nasta'inu a travel-specific account of divine aid."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e37be40e2be78db2e03d", "ref": "WLC:Ezra.8.23", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fasting and entreating God for the journey", "note": "The community fasts and entreats God for the safe journey, and God answers; it supplies a concrete liturgical continuation of asking help before setting out."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b03ce6cc0235a6897ddb", "ref": "WLC:Gen.21.18", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "q-w-m B002; arise and hold a hand", "note": "Genesis tells Hagar to arise (Hebrew qumi) and to hold the boy's hand in the wilderness; the genuine q-w-m cognate joins the source's uprightness to hand-held aid."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cb9777f56e0ce9b88526", "ref": "WLC:Gen.28.20", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "traveler asks guarding, food, and clothing", "note": "Jacob asks that God be with him, guard him on the way, and provide bread and clothing; it gives the vulnerable traveler a prayer for both protection and material support."}
{"connection_id": "BC-853dbe165c3d10aae27d", "ref": "WLC:Gen.47.31", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Worship with a textual variant about support", "note": "WLC's ketiv vocalizes the final noun as “bed,” while its qere reads “staff”; only the qere makes Jacob's worship explicitly lean on a support, so this verse should preserve the distinction rather than silently treating the staff as WLC's written reading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d517d4ad46764224bcfc", "ref": "WLC:Hab.3.19", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God gives strength and sure feet", "note": "God is the speaker's strength, makes the feet like a deer's, and enables walking on heights; it closely combines strength, gait, and difficult terrain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a26d3fd6aa5e24b9a68c", "ref": "WLC:Hos.11.3", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Arms that teach a child to walk", "note": "YHWH says he taught Ephraim to walk and took them in his arms, though they did not recognize that he healed them; the image presents guidance and support as care a dependent person may not even perceive."}
{"connection_id": "BC-de60cb05ffc90fe9b011", "ref": "WLC:Hos.11.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A lifted yoke and food for the dependent", "note": "YHWH describes leading Ephraim with human kindness, lifting the yoke from its jaws, and bending down to feed it; it adds gentle, practical care for a dependent being to the commentary's exhausted mount and traveler."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5f046a70a211bd30998d", "ref": "WLC:Isa.26.7", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A level path for the righteous", "note": "The prophet says the path of the righteous is level and God makes it smooth; this sharpens the commentary's image of guidance into a road made passable for the walker."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1175e8f6e171997f8129", "ref": "WLC:Isa.26.7", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "level path of the righteous", "note": "The path of the righteous is level because God makes it level; it supplies a Hebrew formulation in which moral rightness and an even route converge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3aa473c1ca75c7cc3af9", "ref": "WLC:Isa.30.21", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Direction when turning right or left", "note": "The people hear a voice behind them saying, “This is the way; walk in it,” when they turn right or left; it uniquely connects guidance with the commentary's side-to-side swaying walker."}
{"connection_id": "BC-03e769daeec7e320db23", "ref": "WLC:Isa.30.21", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "this is the way; right or left", "note": "A voice tells the traveler, This is the way, walk in it, when turning right or left; it answers the commentary's swaying walker with directional guidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bb55f0f225267b99aa32", "ref": "WLC:Isa.33.22", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "מֶלֶךְ, king and rescuer", "note": "The source commentary labels Arabic م ل ك for مَٰلِكِ; Hebrew מֶלֶךְ (melekh, root מלך) appears as YHWH is named Israel's judge, lawgiver, and king who saves, joining royal authority with the rescue sought by the dependent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c03c533379216a2b6fa8", "ref": "WLC:Isa.33.22", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "m-l-k B001/B005; king who saves", "note": "The source labels malik with m-l-k; Isaiah's melek is the cognate king term and names the LORD as king who saves us, tying sovereignty to needed deliverance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-806a8d3940cb52ea6646", "ref": "WLC:Isa.35.3", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Weak hands and knees strengthened", "note": "The prophet tells the community to strengthen slack hands and make firm weak knees; this names the bodily weakness that the commentary gives to its traveler before he can walk uprightly."}
{"connection_id": "BC-697af669c7d9a5367671", "ref": "WLC:Isa.35.3", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "strengthen weak hands and knees", "note": "The prophetic command to strengthen weak hands and make feeble knees firm directly shares the commentary's debilitated body before it can walk well."}
{"connection_id": "BC-faccee3d6c9d7a747acb", "ref": "WLC:Isa.35.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Words that steady the fearful", "note": "The community is told to speak courage to fearful hearts because God comes to save; alongside the commentary's physical supports, this shows encouragement as aid to the weak."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a5c4229316e6eb1aa424", "ref": "WLC:Isa.35.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The lame walk again", "note": "The lame leap like a deer when the wilderness blossoms; this turns the commentary's halted, exhausted movement into restored mobility."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e8d590989306e8106ebe", "ref": "WLC:Isa.35.8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A safe highway for the redeemed", "note": "Isaiah continues the restoration scene with a highway called the holy way, on which even the travelers will not stray; it supplies the safe, morally directed road that follows the weak hands and knees in the same chapter."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b948d5ec72dcb26f419b", "ref": "WLC:Isa.35.8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "highway; walkers do not go astray", "note": "The Way of Holiness is a highway on which even inexperienced walkers do not go astray; it brings bodily frailty and a protected route into one prophetic scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-289f62310e2d741ee149", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The shepherd carries and gently leads", "note": "YHWH gathers lambs in his arm, carries them close, and gently leads those with young; the verse gives a concrete image of help adjusted to a traveler's weakness."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-d6675eaf0d88182a2185", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.29", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "power for the faint", "note": "God gives power to the faint and strength to the powerless, directly matching the source's weak traveler who cannot proceed unaided."}
{"connection_id": "BC-382afe3229b9a8a29bed", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.31", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Strength for walking and running", "note": "Those who wait for YHWH renew strength and walk without fainting; the verse supplies endurance for the commentary's weakened traveler, while its running image differs from the commentary's calm, non-fugitive gait."}
{"connection_id": "BC-18642a6d4a9872c6093b", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.31", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "renewed strength; walk without fainting", "note": "Those who wait for the LORD receive renewed strength and walk without fainting; it gives the commentary's aided and composed walking an explicit divine outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ee15b6e57b7b202585e5", "ref": "WLC:Isa.41.10", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Upheld by God's hand", "note": "YHWH tells fearful Israel not to fear, promises help, and says he will uphold them with his righteous hand; the bodily act of holding someone up closely matches the commentary's dependent walker."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fce35be1b12912d8e88c", "ref": "WLC:Isa.41.10", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "strengthen, help, uphold", "note": "God promises to strengthen, help, and uphold with the righteous right hand; the three actions closely mirror aid becoming upright stability."}
{"connection_id": "BC-baa27fb71649be5e48ad", "ref": "WLC:Isa.41.13", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "holding the right hand; divine help", "note": "God takes Israel's right hand and says, I help you; it fuses the commentary's hand-supported traveler with its explicit request for help."}
{"connection_id": "BC-97468a215fb6aa243065", "ref": "WLC:Isa.42.1", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "'ayn B007/B011; upheld servant", "note": "The source labels na'budu with the 'ayn-b-d root; Isaiah calls the chosen eved God's servant and says God upholds him, a cognate service-and-support pairing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-639e0221f9311faf47c7", "ref": "WLC:Isa.42.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God leads on an unknown road", "note": "YHWH promises to lead the blind by roads they have not known, make rough places level, and not forsake them; the passage joins guidance with hands-on provision for a dependent traveler."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f7a30a0121f7165ec6b2", "ref": "WLC:Isa.42.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "leading the blind; rough ground level", "note": "God leads blind people on an unknown way and makes rough places level; it extends the source's guide beyond a front-runner to aid for those unable to navigate alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d1af9e29dc6b9e8c3e9e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.46.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "carry, bear, and save through frailty", "note": "God promises to carry and bear people even into old age and gray hair; it supplies a sustained divine counterpart to the source's body that needs to be held up."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cf59c600ba1138370f04", "ref": "WLC:Isa.48.17", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God leads in the way one should go", "note": "God teaches what is beneficial and leads the people in the way they should go; it is a direct prophetic response to the plea ihdina."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d2e370c283b8058451e1", "ref": "WLC:Isa.49.10", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mercy leads and guides to water", "note": "The hungry and thirsty are protected from scorching because the merciful one leads and guides them to springs; it gives the source's needy traveler both relief and a destination."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71f6aa9b5fa04d34a4f2", "ref": "WLC:Isa.50.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A word that sustains the weary", "note": "The servant says God gives him a trained tongue to sustain the weary with a word; this adds speech as support alongside the commentary's shoulders, upright body, and calm gait."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b7d9a46db39d4ed13342", "ref": "WLC:Isa.57.14", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "build up the way; remove obstruction", "note": "God commands that a road be built and every obstruction removed from the people's way; it turns the commentary's support and wall imagery into preparation for safe passage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d6de43efb139ec961dc", "ref": "WLC:Isa.58.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "continuous guidance; strengthened body", "note": "The LORD continually guides, satisfies need in parched places, and makes the bones strong; this joins the commentary's need, support, and restored movement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b1dba8db189e8ce28e93", "ref": "WLC:Jer.17.8", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A tree rooted by water", "note": "The person who trusts YHWH is like a tree planted by water whose roots reach the stream and whose leaf stays green in drought; this gives a living image for the commentary's tree standing on its roots, with hidden nourishment preserving stability."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2f7f95f2f1b2949e6d9b", "ref": "WLC:Jer.31.9", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A straight road without stumbling", "note": "YHWH leads returning Israelites to streams of water by a straight road on which they will not stumble; the promised route closely joins guidance, sustenance, and secure walking as in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-48789cff3765a1c205c9", "ref": "WLC:Jer.31.9", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "straight path without stumbling", "note": "God leads the returning people by watercourses on a straight path where they will not stumble; this is an unusually close pairing of divine leading, straightness, and safe feet."}
{"connection_id": "BC-58e70abdfc3568ae4f7e", "ref": "WLC:Jer.6.16", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Ask for the good way", "note": "At the crossroads, the people are told to ask for the ancient paths, find the good way, and walk in it for rest; this makes seeking guidance an explicit act, though Jeremiah's audience refuses the invitation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-245dc6fd0c6e7f11a5be", "ref": "WLC:Job.29.15", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "feet for the lame", "note": "Job says he was feet to the lame; the figure makes another person capable of movement, as the source's helpers make a weak walker mobile."}
{"connection_id": "BC-70fc8fb0e5f4e737dcb7", "ref": "WLC:Job.4.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ק־ו־ם, raising the one who stumbles", "note": "The source commentary labels Arabic ق و م (q-w-m) for stopping and standing upright; Job's Hebrew יְקִימוּן (yəqîmûn, from קום) says Job's words used to raise a stumbling person and strengthen weak knees, a cognate use that restores rather than halts movement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8b0745b66c1cd5b5fe48", "ref": "WLC:Job.4.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "upholding the stumbling; feeble knees", "note": "The speaker's words uphold one who stumbles and make weak knees firm; this supplies a verbal and social analogue to the bodily support in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7ff65ea016437f52381f", "ref": "WLC:Josh.3.4", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guide on a way not traveled before", "note": "The ark goes ahead so Israel will know the way to go because it has not passed that way before; it is a precise narrative of a guide for people unable to orient themselves alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d3fc9ef5d32adb816505", "ref": "WLC:Lev.19.14", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "do not place a stumbling block before the blind", "note": "The law prohibits placing a stumbling block before the blind; it gives the source's guided weak traveler a negative ethical counterpart, removing what causes a fall."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8f86a16a4f97500e653f", "ref": "WLC:Mic.4.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "assemble the lame and afflicted", "note": "God will gather the lame, the outcast, and those afflicted; it makes the physically impaired people in the source an object of restoration rather than abandonment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d81b89aedf4440516861", "ref": "WLC:Mic.4.7", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lame remnant made a strong nation", "note": "The lame are made a remnant and the outcast a strong nation; this gives the commentary's movement from weakness to solidity a collective prophetic form."}
{"connection_id": "BC-531996d0daef3cf5bcdf", "ref": "WLC:Neh.9.21", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Feet sustained through forty years", "note": "In a communal confession, the people say God sustained them in the wilderness for forty years, their clothes did not wear out and their feet did not swell; it makes divine aid concrete in the body of travelers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e1bc0f707c03b87e38f0", "ref": "WLC:Prov.19.2", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hasty feet lose the way", "note": "The proverb says that one who hastens with the feet misses the way; it supplies a wisdom contrast to the source's calm, non-panicked good walking."}
{"connection_id": "BC-74a6a60b734cda20e076", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.23", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Walking securely without stumbling", "note": "The pupil will walk securely and his foot will not stumble; this states the bodily result of the steady path and support developed in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c2fc3f8083d7a432cda8", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.23", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walk securely; foot does not stumble", "note": "The wise person walks securely and does not stumble; it gives the commentary's restored gait a wisdom counterpart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-16e3b84af7f6a889fb28", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.26", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Confidence that keeps the foot", "note": "YHWH is the pupil's confidence and keeps his foot from being caught; the verse makes divine reliance a protection for the body's movement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-520f13204f684b5398f5", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.5", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Do not rely on your own understanding", "note": "The proverb tells the pupil not to lean on his own understanding but to trust YHWH; this makes dependence explicit beside the commentary's image of a traveler who cannot support himself, though the proverb speaks metaphorically."}
{"connection_id": "BC-54caa33186b88b34c9cc", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God makes the path straight", "note": "The proverb says to acknowledge YHWH in all one's ways and he will make the paths straight; this is a direct formulation of asking God to guide one along a straight way."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d6821729c76538bd966", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.6", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "making paths straight", "note": "Acknowledging God results in paths made straight; it is a concise wisdom analogue to the communal petition for the straight path."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ce96ac90ad2cc5c72477", "ref": "WLC:Prov.4.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Taught the way of wisdom", "note": "The speaker says he has taught the way of wisdom and led his pupil in upright tracks; this gives a concrete teacher-and-traveler counterpart to asking for guidance on a straight way."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7f8c88e00ee7abd563ce", "ref": "WLC:Prov.4.11", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "led in paths of uprightness", "note": "The teacher leads the pupil in the way of wisdom and paths of uprightness; it joins route-guidance to the moral and bodily verticality heard in mustaqim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c88b7b54b6df93f4192c", "ref": "WLC:Prov.4.12", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Walking without stumbling", "note": "The pupil's stride will not be hampered and he will not stumble when running; this describes the bodily result of the guidance and steadiness developed in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4fcdad8fd4232b2c2565", "ref": "WLC:Prov.4.12", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unhampered steps; no stumble in running", "note": "The instructed walker has unhampered steps and does not stumble even when running; it gives a contrast to the source's impaired gait and its recovery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4518f20890dc4c325b2e", "ref": "WLC:Prov.4.26", "target": "sec11", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Make the path level for your feet", "note": "The wisdom teacher tells the pupil to make a level path for his feet and make all his ways sure; this continues the same chapter's taught walk with an explicit act of stabilizing the road."}
